163	/// Returns true if the data looks safe to paste. Data is considered
164	/// unsafe if it contains any of the following:
165	///
166	/// - `\n` or `\r`: Line terminators can be used to inject commands.
167	///   A carriage return is what the Enter key sends, and an unbracketed
168	///   paste converts newlines to carriage returns anyway, so the two are
169	///   equivalent on the input stream. Other terminals that warn on
170	///   multi-line pastes (iTerm2, Windows Terminal, kitty) treat both
171	///   bytes as line terminators as well.
172	/// - `\x1b[201~`: This is the end of a bracketed paste. This can be used
173	///   to exit a bracketed paste and inject commands.
174	///
175	/// We consider any scenario unsafe regardless of current terminal state.
176	/// For example, even if bracketed paste mode is not active, we still
177	/// consider `\x1b[201~` unsafe. The existence of these types of bytes
178	/// should raise suspicion that the producer of the paste data is
179	/// acting strangely.
180	pub fn isSafe(data: []const u8) bool {
181	    return std.mem.indexOfAny(u8, data, "\r\n") == null and
182	        std.mem.indexOf(u8, data, "\x1b[201~") == null;
183	}
184	
185	/// Returns true if the data looks safe to paste given how it will be
186	/// encoded. This is the terminal-state-aware counterpart of `isSafe`:
187	///
188	/// - Bracketed (mode 2004 on): the program receives the data as one
189	///   framed unit, so line terminators are fine. The data is unsafe only if it
190	///   contains the end of the frame (`\x1b[201~`), which would let the
191	///   rest of the data escape the frame and inject commands.
192	/// - Unbracketed: the same rule as `isSafe`.
193	///
194	/// Callers wanting the conservative rule regardless of terminal state
195	/// should use `isSafe` instead.
196	pub fn isSafeWith(data: []const u8, opts: Options) bool {
197	    if (opts.bracketed) return std.mem.indexOf(u8, data, bracketed_suffix) == null;
198	    return isSafe(data);
199	}
